syntax:
For a class with virtual functions:

// Compiler-generated layout
class ClassName {
    // Data members
    ...
    // Implicit pointer to vtable (vptr)
    // vptr is not explicitly written in code but exists in memory layout
}

// Vtable structure (conceptual)
// An array of function pointers, one for each virtual function
// For example:
// vtable for ClassName: [ &ClassName::virtualFunc1, &ClassName::virtualFunc2, ... ]

code_example:
class Base {
public:
    virtual void foo() { /* base implementation */ }
};
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void foo() override { /* derived implementation */ }
};
// Object memory layout:
// Derived obj: [ data members | vptr ]
// vptr points to Derived's vtable: [ &Derived::foo ]
// When calling basePtr->foo(), the program uses vptr to locate the correct function
// at runtime, enabling dynamic dispatch.
